    "body": "## Summary\n\nImplements **ADR-024: Hover-Match Transition Check** — the final fix in the hover performance arc ADR-021 opened.\n\n**Wrapper-element hovers on Wikipedia: 900ms (ADR-022 baseline) → ~7.5ms.** ~120× cumulative over three ADRs. Link hovers unchanged at ~18ms. All claims measured, none predicted.\n\n## The arc in numbers\n\n| Hover type | ADR-022 | ADR-023 | ADR-024 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Link hover | 14-25ms | 14-25ms | **~18ms** (unchanged) |\n| Wrapper hover | 850-932ms | 340-460ms | **~7.5ms** |\n\n**~120× wrapper improvement over three ADRs.** All sub-perceptual now (well under the ~50ms human-noticeability threshold).\n\n## What this PR ships\n\n### Commit 1 (`6ab55bf`): Extract the helper\n\n- `collect_anchor_hover_selectors(ua, author)` — pulls anchor-position `:hover` selectors once per restyle\n- `matches_any_anchor_hover(el, state, anchor_hover)` — the per-element match check, extracted from `compute_hover_sensitive`'s inner loop\n- 3 new tests including the **`<span>`-inside-`<a>` subtle case** (ancestor `:hover` transitions when a descendant is hovered)\n\nMechanical refactor, behavior-preserving. Existing ground-truth test (`only <a> elements flagged`) still passes through the new path.\n\n### Commit 2 (`c40e394`): The transition check\n\nIn `restyle()`'s Level 1 filtered walk:\n\n```cpp\nbool old_match = matches_any_anchor_hover(el, old_state, anchor_hover);\nbool new_match = matches_any_anchor_hover(el, state_, anchor_hover);\nif (old_match == new_match) continue;\n// cascade el\n```\n\n`anchor_hover` and `old_state` built once per transition, not per element.\n\nAlgorithmic guard tightened: wrapper-hover `apply_rule` count drops from ~170 (ADR-023) to **0** (nothing transitions, nothing cascades). Threshold `<1000` → `<20`.\n\nNew correctness test: hover `<a>`, then hover the ancestor `<div>`. The `<a>`'s `a:hover` match transitions true→false — it **must** be re-cascaded to revert its hover color. A per-hovered-element check would leave a stale color; this test catches that.\n\n### Commit 3 (`d2a68ff`): Docs\n\nADR-024 Implementation section with measured distribution, three-ADR comparison table, honest overperformance explanation, and **Meta-lesson-v2**.\n\n## Measured performance (Wikipedia, 799KB, 7,871 elements, 158 rules, debug -O0)\n\n| Transition | `flagged` | `transitioned` | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Wrapper (`<html>`, `<body>`) | 2,191 | **0** | ~7.5ms (collect 6.5 + check 0.9) |\n| Link (`<section>` ↔ `<a>`) | 123 | **1** | ~18ms (0.6 + 0.4 + propagate 12 + walk 5) |\n\n**Transition counts match ADR-024's pre-implementation predictions exactly** (`flagged=2,191 transitioned=0`; `flagged=123 transitioned=1`). That's the measurement-first discipline working — the numbers in the spec were grounded, and the implementation confirmed them.\n\n## The honest overperformance (Meta-lesson-v2)\n\nADR-024's Decision section predicted **~25ms** for wrapper hover. Measured: **~7.5ms**. Off by ~3× in the pleasant direction.\n\n**Root cause:** the ~25ms arithmetic was `walk 5 + check 4 + cascade 0 + propagate 12 + restyle_walk 5`. It assumed propagate and restyle_walk **always** run. But ADR-022's implementation already has a `did_work` early-return: when zero elements cascade, propagate and walk are skipped. For wrapper hover, `transitioned=0` → early return fires → ~17ms of work skipped.\n\nCorrectness unaffected (no cache entry changed → nothing to propagate, nothing to repaint). The early-return is pre-existing and safe.\n\n**Meta-lesson-v2** (documented in the ADR): ADR-024 followed ADR-023's Meta-lesson — measure before predicting — yet was still 3× off. The missing piece: **the prediction didn't model the actual code path**. Component measurements are necessary but not sufficient; you also need to know which components *execute*.\n\nThis refines ADR-023's Meta-lesson rather than contradicting it. Both failure modes point to the same principle: **predictions are only as good as the model of reality they're built from**. ADR-023's model omitted the scale; ADR-024's omitted the control flow.\n\n## Test count\n\n- Before ADR-024: 267 cases, 1140 assertions\n- After Commit 1: 270 cases, 1157 assertions (+3 helper tests)\n- After Commit 2: **271 cases, 1185 assertions** (+1 ancestor-hover correctness test)\n- Zero warnings, zero failures\n\n## Verification checklist\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Wrapper hover ~7.5ms on Wikipedia | ✅ measured |\n| Link hover ~18ms (no regression) | ✅ measured |\n| Transition counts match predictions | ✅ (2,191/0; 123/1) |\n| Cache-equality invariant preserved | ✅ existing + new tests |\n| `<a>`→`<div>` hover reverts `<a>` color | ✅ new correctness test |\n| Algorithmic guard wrapper `<20` calls | ✅ (measured 0) |\n| `<span>`-in-`<a>` ancestor transition handled | ✅ test |\n\n## Not included (deferred)\n\n- **Level 2 (compound `:hover`) optimization** — unchanged from ADR-023; no current use case\n- **`prev_hovered_`-aware subtree-walk skip** — walk still runs (~6.5ms on wrapper); could be eliminated but 7.5ms is already sub-perceptual\n- **Release build measurements** — debug numbers here; release expected 5-10× faster still\n\n## ADR\n\n`docs/decisions/024-hover-transition-check.md` — status now **Implemented**, with Implementation section (measured distribution + honest overperformance note) and **Meta-lesson-v2**.\n",
